148      COMMENTS                                                26NDS    202601
148    H TYP=FUL$AUT=N. NICA$CIT=NDS 208, 1 (2026)$CUT=17-Jan-2026$             
148   c  TITL$Nuclear data sheets for A=148                                     
148   c  AUTH$N. NICA                                                           
148   c  INST$Cyclotron Institute, Texas A&M University,                        
148  #c       College Station, Texas 77843-3366 USA.                            
148   C  CIT$NDS 208, 1 (2026)                                                  
148   c  ABST$The experimental results published before January 2026 from the   
148  2c  various reaction and decay studies leading to nuclides of Z=54 to      
148  2c  Z=70, {+148}Xe, {+148}Cs, {+148}Ba, {+148}La, {+148}Ce, {+148}Pr,      
148  2c  {+148}Nd, {+148}Pm, {+148}Sm, {+148}Eu, {+148}Gd, {+148}Tb, {+148}Dy,  
148  2c  {+148}Ho, {+148}Er, {+148}Tm, {+148}Yb in the A=148 mass chain have    
148  2c  been reviewed and the data are presented together with adopted level   
148  2c  schemes and properties. This work supersedes the previous evaluation   
148  2c  of the A=148 nuclides by published by N. Nica in Nuclear Data Sheets   
148  2c  117, 1 (2014)                                                          
148   c  CUT$Literature available up to Jan 17, 2026 has been been considered   
148  2c  with the primary source of bibliographic information being the Nuclear 
148  2c  Sciences References (NSR) database (2011Pr03, 2014Pr09) available at   
148  2c  www.nndc.bnl.gov.                                                      
148   c  ACKN$The author is very grateful to the previous evaluators of this    
148  2c  mass chain, M.R. Bhat (2000Bh03), L.K. Peker (1990Pe01,1984Pe14),      
148  2c  B.Harmatz, J.R.Shepard (1977Ha16) The author is also grateful to B.    
148  2c  Pritychenko (BNL) with his collaborators, who contributed to the NSR   
148  2c  reference database which made possible this evaluation, to B. Singh    
148  2c  (McMaster University) and the other compilers who made available the   
148  2c  XUNDL files, as well as to JoAnn Totans for providing the copies of    
148  2c  many rare references in support of this nuclear data evaluation.  I    
148  2c  also thank to the reviewer of this work, and to E.A.  McCutchan (BNL)  
148  2c  for the editorial work of the manuscript of the evaluation, as well as 
148  2c  to D. Brown, Head of the NNDC (BNL) for support. Finally I pay tribute 
148  2c  to the memory of T.W. Burrows, my mentor evaluator.                    
148   c  FUND$This work is supported by the Office of Nuclear Physics, Office   
148  xc  of Science, U.S. Department of Energy under Grant No.                  
148  xc  DE-FG02-93ER40773                                                      

148XE    ADOPTED LEVELS                                          26NDS    202601
148XE  H TYP=FUL$AUT=N. NICA$CIT=NDS 208, 1 (2026)$CUT=17-Jan-2026$             
148XE  Q 8260      SY 4320   SY 14740  SY -4850  SY    2021WA16                 
148XE cQ $|DQ(|b{+-})=300, |DS(n)=360, |DS(p)=420, |DQ(|a)=420 (syst,2021Wa16)  
148XE cQ $S(2n)=6840 {I300}, Q(|b{+-}n)=5200 {I300} (syst,2021Wa16)             
148XE c  2020Wu04 compiled for XUNDL database by B. Singh (McMaster)            
148XE c  2020Wu04: {+148}Xe nuclide produced at the RIBF-RIKEN facility in      
148XE2c  {+9}Be({+238}U,F) reaction at E=345 MeV/nucleon at beam intensity of   
148XE3c  5pnA. Fission fragments identified by magnetic rigidity (B|r) and      
148XE4c  energy loss (|DE) time-of-flight (tof), with BigRIPS spectrometer,     
148XE5c  which determines atomic Z and mass-to-charge ratio A/Q, (Q=charge state
148XE6c  of the ions). Separated nuclei were implanted at 100 ions/s rate of in 
148XE8c  the beta counting system WAS3ABi (stack of five Double Sided           
148XEAc  Silicon Strip Detectors (DSSSDs)). The WAS3ABi setup was surrounded    
148XEBc  by EURICA cluster array (84 HPGe |g-ray detectors). Measured half-life 
148XEDc  of separated and implanted ions by fitting the time distribution of    
148XEHc  the correlated decay curves. Comparison of measured half-lives with    
148XEIc  FRDM+QRPA (2003), FRDM+QRPA (2019), KTUY+GT2, RHB+pn-RQRPA, and        
148XEIc  DF+CQRPA theoretical calculations.                                     
148XE c  2010Oh02: {+148}Xe nuclide identified in Be({+238}U,F) and             
148XE2c  Pb({+238}U,F) reactions with {+238}U{+86+} beam energy of              
148XE3c  345 MeV/nucleon produced by the cascade operation of the RBIF          
148XE4c  accelerator complex of the linear accelerator RILAC and four           
148XE5c  cyclotrons RRC, fRC, IRC and SRC. Identification of {+148}Xe nuclei was
148XE6c  made on the basis of magnetic rigidity, time-of-flight and energy loss 
148XE7c  of the fragments using BigRIPS fragment separator. Experiments         
148XE8c  performed at RIKEN facility.                                           
148XE c  Based on A/Q spectrum and Z versus A/Q plot (Q=charge state), one count
148XE2c  was assigned to {+148}Xe isotope.                                      
148XE d  Structure calculations:                                                
148XE d  1984Na22: calculated equilibrium shapes                                
148XE d  1986Au02: calculated two-neutron separation energies                   
148XE d  1988So08: calculated equilibrium deformation, energy.                  
148XE d  1988Ha24: calculated atomic masses                                     
148XE d  1992Na07: calculated equilibrium deformation as function of            
148XE2d  rotational frequency, shape change features                            
148XE d  1994Ma02: calculated levels, energy splitting, transition probabilities
148XE  L 0              0+                85 MS  15                             
148XE2 L %B-=100 $ %B-N=?                                                       
148XE cL %B-$only the |b{+-} decay mode is expected and detected, followed by   
148XE2cL |b-delayed neutron decay                                               
148XE cL T$from 2020Wu04, fitting the time distribution of                      
148XE2cL (implanted ions) |b-correlated decay curves to the sum of activities   
148XE3cL of parent nuclei, daughter nuclei, grand-daughter nuclei, |b{+-}n      
148XE4cL daughter and grand-daughter nuclei, and a constant background.         
148XE2cL Other: > 0.4 |ms, lower limit from time-of-flight in 2010Oh02, as      
148XE2cL communicated to B. Singh by T. Kubo (private communication, July 14,   
148XE3cL 2010).                                                                 
148XE cL $ Measured |s=70 pb (2010Oh02), systematic uncertainty|?30%            
148XE cL $ Probability of misidentification of {+148}Xe isotope=0.46% (2010Oh02)
148XE cL $Calculated %|b{+-}n=10.0 (2019Mo01); 9.6 (2002Pf04)                   

