148ER c  1982No08: identification and production of {+148}Er isotope            
148ER cG E,RI$From {+92}Mo({+58}Ni,2p|g)                                        
148ER cG M$ From |g-asymmetry data in {+92}Mo({+58}Ni,2p|g)                     
148ER cG M(Y)$ From |g-asymmetry, |DJ=2, quadrupole (most likely E2). Mult=(E2) 
148ER2cG is assigned to transitions in the yrast sequence from g.s. to (10+)    
148ER3cG isomer, based on definite E2 for (10+) to (8+) transition and stretched
148ER4cG quadrupoles to other four transitions                                  
148ER cG M(Z)$ From |g-asymmetry, |DJ=1, dipole+quadrupole (most likely M1+E2). 
148ER2cG In a few cases transition is indicated as |DJ=0                        
148ER cG M(U)$ From |g-asymmetry, |DJ=1, dipole (most likely E1)                
148ER cL $ In the table below, observed levels in {+148}Er are classified in    
148ER2cL terms of spherical shell-model configurations while comparing with the 
148ER3cL known states in N=80 isotones and other neighboring nuclides           
148ER4cL ({+146}Dy,{+144}Gd,{+148}Dy and {+150}Er). The valence orbitals        
148ER5cL are: h{-11/2}, s{-1/2}, d{-3/2}, g{-7/2} 2-neutron holes; 2-4 proton   
148ER6cL orbitals (mainly h{-11/2}) outside the {+146}Gd core. Six-qp           
148ER7cL configurations are expected above 7 MeV, but 2001Ro15 did not identify 
148ER8cL specific levels for these configurations due to high level density     
